{"content_hash": "55a57428c7a82989", "version_id": 236, "article_id": 24, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "ペストはまだあります。アフリカとアメリカで、毎年、人が病気になります", "title_zh": "鼠疫其实一直没消失，非洲和美国几乎年年都有人感染", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2026/the-plague-never-went.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 311, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "ペストは、むかし大ぜいの人が死んだ病気です。でも、いまもまだあります。アフリカやアメリカで、毎年、人がかかります。", "lead_zh": "鼠疫是一种过去让很多人丧命的病。但它现在仍然存在。在非洲和美国，每年都有人感染。", "preview": "ペストは、むかしヨーロッパで大ぜいの人が死んだ病気です。いまも、まだあります。でも、いまはくすりでなおせます。", "preview_zh": "鼠疫是一种过去在欧洲让很多人丧命的病。它现在仍然存在。不过现在可以用药治好。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 226, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/audio/24/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 66.75, "size_bytes": 404208, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 201, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/data/versions/236.json", "paragraphs": ["ペストは、むかしヨーロッパで大ぜいの人が死んだ病気です。いまも、まだあります。でも、いまはくすりでなおせます。", "1990年から、人の病気はアフリカで多いです。とくにコンゴとマダガスカルです。アメリカでは、毎年、七人ぐらいです。おもに西のいなかです。", "ペストは、ねずみなどのどうぶつのあいだにいます。ノミが人をかむと、人も病気になります。", "ペストには三つのしゅるいがあります。リンパせつがはれるもの、はいがわるくなるもの、ちがわるくなるもの です。", "はやくびょういんへ行くと、ペストはなおります。おそいと、死ぬことがあります。ワクチンもあります。でも、アメリカにはありません。", "ロシアのシベリアで、けんきゅうしょの人がしにました。ロシアの人は「ペストではない」と言いました。でも、けんきゅうしょは、いま入れません。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["鼠疫是一种过去在欧洲让很多人丧命的病。它现在仍然存在。不过现在可以用药治好。", "从1990年起，人类病例大多出现在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年大约有七例，主要在美国西部的乡村地区。", "鼠疫存在于老鼠等动物之间。跳蚤咬了人，人也会得病。", "鼠疫有三种类型：淋巴结肿大的、肺部出问题的，还有血液出问题的。", "早点去医院，鼠疫就能治好。去晚了可能会死。也有疫苗，但美国没有。", "在俄罗斯的西伯利亚，研究所的一名工作人员去世了。俄罗斯方面说“不是鼠疫”。但这家研究所现在被隔离了。"], "body": "ペストは、むかしヨーロッパで大ぜいの人が死んだ病気です。いまも、まだあります。でも、いまはくすりでなおせます。\n\n1990年から、人の病気はアフリカで多いです。とくにコンゴとマダガスカルです。アメリカでは、毎年、七人ぐらいです。おもに西のいなかです。\n\nペストは、ねずみなどのどうぶつのあいだにいます。ノミが人をかむと、人も病気になります。\n\nペストには三つのしゅるいがあります。リンパせつがはれるもの、はいがわるくなるもの、ちがわるくなるもの です。\n\nはやくびょういんへ行くと、ペストはなおります。おそいと、死ぬことがあります。ワクチンもあります。でも、アメリカにはありません。\n\nロシアのシベリアで、けんきゅうしょの人がしにました。ロシアの人は「ペストではない」と言いました。でも、けんきゅうしょは、いま入れません。", "body_zh": "鼠疫是一种过去在欧洲让很多人丧命的病。它现在仍然存在。不过现在可以用药治好。\n\n从1990年起，人类病例大多出现在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年大约有七例，主要在美国西部的乡村地区。\n\n鼠疫存在于老鼠等动物之间。跳蚤咬了人，人也会得病。\n\n鼠疫有三种类型：淋巴结肿大的、肺部出问题的，还有血液出问题的。\n\n早点去医院，鼠疫就能治好。去晚了可能会死。也有疫苗，但美国没有。\n\n在俄罗斯的西伯利亚，研究所的一名工作人员去世了。俄罗斯方面说“不是鼠疫”。但这家研究所现在被隔离了。", "text": "ペストは、むかしヨーロッパで大ぜいの人が死んだ病気です。いまも、まだあります。でも、いまはくすりでなおせます。 1990年から、人の病気はアフリカで多いです。とくにコンゴとマダガスカルです。アメリカでは、毎年、七人ぐらいです。おもに西のいなかです。 ペストは、ねずみなどのどうぶつのあいだにいます。ノミが人をかむと、人も病気になります。 ペストには三つのしゅるいがあります。リンパせつがはれるもの、はいがわるくなるもの、ちがわるくなるもの です。 はやくびょういんへ行くと、ペストはなおります。おそいと、死ぬことがあります。ワクチンもあります。でも、アメリカにはありません。 ロシアのシベリアで、けんきゅうしょの人がしにました。ロシアの人は「ペストではない」と言いました。でも、けんきゅうしょは、いま入れません。", "vocab": [{"word": "ペスト", "pos": "noun", "zh": "鼠疫", "note": "外来语，指这种传染病"}, {"word": "病気", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疾病", "note": "常用词，N5 基础词汇"}, {"word": "くすり", "pos": "noun", "zh": "药", "note": "常写作「薬」，此处用平假名"}, {"word": "ねずみ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "老鼠", "note": "动物名，常用平假名"}, {"word": "ノミ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "跳蚤", "note": "外来语，传播鼠疫的虫子"}, {"word": "毎年", "pos": "noun", "zh": "每年", "note": "表示频率的时间词"}, {"word": "なおる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "治好、痊愈", "note": "五段动词，常写作「治る」"}, {"word": "ワクチン", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疫苗", "note": "外来语，医疗用语"}], "title_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year", "summary_original": "The plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.", "body_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year\nby The Associated Press\nedited by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\nreputable news agency\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nThe plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.\nSince the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially Congo and Madagascar, according to the\nWorld Health Organization\n.\nThere are an average of about seven human cases per year in the U.S.,\naccording to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\n, mostly in rural areas of the American West.\nA worker at a plague research institute in Siberia recently died of pneumonia.\nThe case has raised alarm\nin Russia after unconfirmed media reports suggested she might have died of pneumonic plague. Russian authorities say testing showed no evidence of plague, but the research institute has been quarantined.\n\"People think the plague is gone, it was something that occurred 500 years ago. It's not. It's here amongst us,\" said Simon Clarke of the University of Reading in the U.K. \"Unless you get rid of those wild animals, which of course is not possible, then you're not going to get rid of the bacteria.\"\nSurprised to hear the plague is still around? Here's what to know.\nPlague can attack lymph nodes, lungs and blood\nPeople in Siberia have been dying of plague for at least 5,500 years. The earliest evidence of a form of the disease was\nfound in teeth from ancient hunter-gatherers\nnorth of the region's Lake Baikal.\nPneumonic plague and the more well-known bubonic plague—also called the Black Death—are caused by the same bacteria, called Yersinia pestis.\nThe bacteria spread naturally among rodents like prairie dogs and rats. Fleas that jump from infected animals and bite people can spread the disease.\nThe bubonic plague is the most common form of the infection. It causes painfully swollen lymph nodes that are most commonly found in the groin, armpit and neck, called buboes. Other symptoms include sudden high fever and chills, headaches, and pain in the abdomen, legs and arms, according to the Cleveland Clinic.\nThere are two other forms of the plague: pneumonic plague, which can be caused by inhaling the bacteria, infects the lungs, while septicemic plague is an infection in the bloodstream. Both are fatal if not treated early, according to the World Health Organization.\nBubonic plague will often develop into the other two forms of plague if untreated.\nPlague can spread through fleas or bodily fluids\nOther than bites from infected fleas, people can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so health experts recommend taking extra care when handling dead or sick animals.\nThe plague can also spread through the respiratory droplets of someone who has pneumonic plague.\n\"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann, MedStar Washington Hospital Center. \"So you really need to be in face-to-face contact with that person to get pneumonic plague.\"\nThat's why \"the chance of this spreading widely in Russia and then extending here to the U.S. will not happen,\" he said.\nAntibiotics can treat plague\nThe plague was never eradicated, but we've gotten better at preventing its spread and treating it in humans.\nWhen treated early with antibiotics, the plague can be cured. The key is to get to a doctor fast—otherwise the plague can be deadly.\nThere is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for people who are at high risk of infection, like laboratory and healthcare workers. There isn't strong evidence that vaccines can work against the pneumonic form.\nThere's no plague vaccine available in the U.S.\nKey medical concepts\nBubonic Plague\nPneumonic Plague\nYersinia pestis\nAntibiotics\nClinical categories\nInfectious diseases\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nWho's behind this story?\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\n© 2026 The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten or redistributed without permission.\nCitation\n:\nThe plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T09:31:25.521123", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "ペスト", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.5, "sm": 125, "em": 500, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "は", "s": 0.512, "e": 0.787, "sm": 512, "em": 787, "cs": 3, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "、", "s": 0.787, "e": 1.075, "sm": 787, "em": 1075, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "むかし", "s": 1.075, "e": 1.487, "sm": 1075, "em": 1487, "cs": 5, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "ヨーロッパ", "s": 1.5, "e": 2.013, "sm": 1500, "em": 2012, "cs": 8, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "で", "s": 2.025, "e": 2.188, "sm": 2025, "em": 2187, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "大ぜい", "s": 2.25, "e": 2.7, "sm": 2250, "em": 2700, "cs": 14, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "の", "s": 2.712, "e": 2.788, "sm": 2712, "em": 2787, "cs": 17, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "人", "s": 2.8, "e": 3.025, "sm": 2800, "em": 3025, "cs": 18, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "が", "s": 3.038, "e": 3.188, "sm": 3037, "em": 3187, "cs": 19, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "死ん", "s": 3.212, "e": 3.487, "sm": 3212, "em": 3487, "cs": 20, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "だ", "s": 3.5, "e": 3.612, "sm": 3500, "em": 3612, "cs": 22, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "病気", "s": 3.625, 